From 2aa33973544520bf6704116f4080ee38342e28c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: (PLAN/178 differential certification)
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 10:48:04 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(bpe): resolve out-of-vocabulary characters like the
 reference

A ByteLevel BPE vocabulary may be missing the entry for a byte. When a
pretoken contains such a character, `Bpe` currently fails the whole
encode with `character 'X' not in vocabulary` (or, on the raw merge
paths, `byte 0xNN has no token in vocabulary`), while the reference
implementation resolves it and keeps going.

Match the reference's resolution order for a character with no
vocabulary entry:

1. byte fallback, when the model enables it and every `<0xXX>` token
   for that character's UTF-8 bytes exists;
2. the model's `unk_token`, when declared and present in the
   vocabulary (honouring `fuse_unk`);
3. otherwise drop the character.

`unk_token` and `fuse_unk` were parsed but never carried into the
runtime model, so step 2 previously had no effect at all; they are now
stored alongside `byte_fallback` and included in `Clone` and `PartialEq`.

The two raw (pre-ByteLevel) merge paths route inputs holding an
unmapped byte through the character engine, so all three entry points
share one resolution policy instead of each having its own error.

Observed on a 120k-entry ByteLevel vocabulary with no token for
carriage return: encoding a document containing "\r" raised
`ValueError: model error: character 'c-with-caron' not in vocabulary`,
where the reference returns the surrounding tokens with the carriage
return dropped. Roughly 3 documents per million in a web corpus are
affected.

Adds seven unit tests covering the drop path, the unk path, `fuse_unk`,
byte-fallback precedence, byte-fallback falling through to unk, raw and
character paths agreeing, and inputs longer than the small-merge limit.
